WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.035
And this is very exciting.
And I did not realize this.

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:04.471 size:88% position:44%
Your daughter is in town
this week because she is

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:06.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
on "Young Sheldon."
-That's right.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:06.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
-She's an actress.
-Yes.

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:11.745
-And she plays Young Sheldon's
mother -- which is crazy because

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:14.347 line:92%
you play Old Sheldon's mother.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:15.682 line:92%
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:17.751 line:92%
That is -- I had no idea --

00:00:17.751 --> 00:00:19.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
-This can't have
ever happened anywhere --

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:20.086 line:92%
-It can never have happened.

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:22.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I know that mothers and
daughters have played mothers

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:25.158 align:start size:94% position:6%
and daughters, and maybe
they've played the same person

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:26.559 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a flashback, maybe.
-Yeah.

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:29.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But this is on two
concurrently running shows.

00:00:29.596 --> 00:00:31.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Both Mary Cooper.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.933 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, and two,
like, different timelines.

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:34.834 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-It's insane.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:36.736 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
So -- it's been fun.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:41.141
Now that "Young Sheldon" exists,
when I do go in every once

00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:44.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a while to do a "Big Bang,"
it really -- I look at --

00:00:44.411 --> 00:00:48.448
I look at Jim differently.
I know him now as the little boy

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:52.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
on "Young Sheldon" and
the other kids on the family

00:00:52.452 --> 00:00:53.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they've cast.
It's just been --

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:56.289
-So, you're retroactively --
-There's layers upon layers.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:59.192 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, I am. I am.
-That's fantastic.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:02.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, congratulations
on your Tony nomination.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:03.663 size:81% position:41% line:92%
This is a fantastic --

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:06.099 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:09.269 align:start size:94% position:6%
Fantastic cast.
Glenda Jackson, Allison Pill.

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:11.738
Were you familiar with the play
when you signed on to do it?

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:14.174 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I had never done an
Edward Albee play before.

00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:18.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had always heard
the title "Three Tall Women,"

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:21.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
had never read it,
and signed on to do it blind

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:24.184
because I wanted to be in
the room with Joe Mantello,

00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:28.388
the director, Glenda Jackson,
the legend, Allison Pill, oh --

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:31.658
who I've been dying to work
with, and also the producer,

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:33.893 align:start size:94% position:6%
Scott Rudin, who,
you know, is --

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.229 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He knows what he's doing.
-Yes, he does.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:36.763 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Yes.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And this is --
I did not realize this about

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:41.801 align:start size:94% position:6%
Glenda Jackson, your co-star.
So, she won two Oscars

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.203 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then she went
into Parliament.

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:45.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Walked away from --
-And then was in Parliament

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:47.173 align:start size:94% position:6%
for 23 years.
-Ran for Parliament.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
Lost the first time, I think.
Ran again.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:51.744 size:75% position:38% line:92%
Served for 23 years.

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:55.315
Left that and then decided
to get back into theater

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:56.950 line:92%
and decided to do "King Lear."

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
She played "King Lear"
in London.

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:00.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Wow.
[ Light laughter ]

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.322 size:81% position:41%
-So, she -- she jumped
right back in again.

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:06.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
And -- now she's on Broadway
starring in this.

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:08.194 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she's fantastic.
-Is it intimidating to work

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
with someone like that
or do you just learn from it

00:02:11.931 --> 00:02:12.632 size:75% position:38% line:92%
and is it wonderful?

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:16.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I thought that
I had pretty good stamina

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.537 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause I've been doing theater
for a long time.

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:21.474
You do the eight shows a week
and I think Glenda could do,

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:22.208 line:92%
like, a circus performer.

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:25.612
I think that she could literally
do about 13 shows a week.

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:27.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, just stack 'em up.
Three shows a day.

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:31.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
She has fantastic stamina.
She's got that voice

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
that carries to
the back row and beyond.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.022 align:start size:94% position:6%
-What an awesome thing
to be around.

00:02:36.022 --> 00:02:37.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-You -- you also found out,

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.759
and congratulations.
You were nominated for an Oscar

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:42.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
this year for "Ladybird,"
in which you were fantastic.

00:02:42.395 --> 00:02:44.030 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:48.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
And -- and I know
they're going to do a prequel

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:51.171
of "Ladybird," and your daughter
is going to play the mom again.

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:52.238 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, probably. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:55.775
-But -- so, you found out
the first day of rehearsal that

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:56.576 line:92%
you were nominated for an Oscar.

00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:59.512
Does that affect how you prepare
for a play on the first day?

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:00.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
Getting, sort of --
-You know, well,

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:03.883
I'll just always remember
it as this fantastic day.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:04.884 line:92%
These kind of days don't happen.

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:08.321
My favorite place to be is in a
rehearsal room on the first day

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:10.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
of rehearsal and start --
open up the script

00:03:10.123 --> 00:03:12.792
and everybody's around the table
and you know you're really going

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:15.061 align:start size:94% position:6%
to dig in and start the work.
I love that.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:17.597
And that morning,
the Oscar nominations came out.

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:20.200
So, when those two came together
on the same day, I mean,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.569 align:start size:94% position:6%
how good does that get?
-Yeah, that's really good.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.337 size:84% position:42%
[ Cheers and applause ]
Really well deserved.

00:03:24.337 --> 00:03:28.741
You have -- you've been doing
theater for a very long time.

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:30.743 align:start size:94% position:6%
As you mentioned, you were one
of the founding members of

00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:32.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
the Steppenwolf troupe
in Chicago.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:35.848
I was lucky enough to see shows
there when I lived there.

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:36.516 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Here you guys are.

00:03:36.516 --> 00:03:38.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is a younger --
people might not recognize.

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:41.221 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's young John Malkovich,
there.

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:41.621 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:44.057 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Gary Sinise, there.
And you were all, pretty much,

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
actors of the same age
when you started it, yeah?

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:47.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right, right, right.
Right.

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:50.063
-And did that make it hard to
figure out who was playing what?

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:51.764 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
You can't find too many plays

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:54.934 align:start size:94% position:6%
that have two, four, six,
eight -- eight actors

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:55.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the same age.
-Yeah.

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:58.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, we ran out quickly.
So what we had to do

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:01.908 align:start size:94% position:6%
was play roles that
we'd never get cast in before.

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:04.510 size:84% position:42%
So, like, I would play
John Malkovich's mother

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.679
in "True West," and then I would
bounce around and play

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:11.117 align:start size:94% position:6%
his niece -- 13-year-old niece
in "Fifth of July."

00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:13.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's Jeff Perry, too.
Joan Allen.

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:16.656
And -- and, so, we got --
we got better that way.

00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:19.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
We challenged ourselves,
and our audiences in Chicago

00:04:19.692 --> 00:04:22.328 align:start size:94% position:6%
rolled with us, you know,
they were really supportive.

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:25.698
They were taken aback at some
of the choices that we did,

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:27.367 line:92%
but they -- they stuck with us.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:29.869
-You -- obviously, you have your
responsibilities as an actor

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:32.639
onstage, but you're also helping
run this theater company?

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:35.275
Was that a difficult time
or was that alt-thrilling?

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:35.942 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-It was -- it was fun.

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:38.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
We just thought
we would do it for a summer.

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:39.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Uh-huh.
-And now I think we're in our

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:41.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
45th season, I'm not sure.
-Unbelievable.

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:45.652 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But we were in a
little church basement up

00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:47.086 line:92%
in Highland Park, Illinois.

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:48.755 line:92%
Everybody just wanted to direct.

00:04:48.755 --> 00:04:51.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
Nobody -- I mean, everybody
wanted to act and be on stage.

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:53.026 size:88% position:44% line:92%
Nobody wanted to direct.

00:04:53.026 --> 00:04:56.896
So, that was the -- the job that
somebody every once in a while

00:04:56.896 --> 00:05:01.367
had to take and also clean the
bathrooms and tear the tickets.

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:03.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Gotcha.
I did not realize this,

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.138
but I've seen --
I've seen your name in the past

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:08.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
listed as a former cast member
of "Saturday Night Live."

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:09.175 align:start size:94% position:6%
In turns out,
you did two things.

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:13.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
In 1981 -- that's -- it's very
hard to even tell it's you.

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:16.382 align:start size:94% position:6%
You did a 'Man on the Street"
bit in 1981.

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:18.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And then you did a piece

00:05:18.685 --> 00:05:21.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
with Catherine O'Hara,
a short film

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is called
"Laurie Has a Story,"

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:24.957 align:start size:94% position:6%
which I watched today,
and it's fantastic.

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.159 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Do you have any memory of it?

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:28.227 size:84% position:42%
-I -- that's funny that
you should say that.

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:30.396 align:start size:94% position:6%
It is like a dream.
I see that there's proof.

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:32.532 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's proof
that I did do these things.

00:05:32.532 --> 00:05:36.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
But in '81, you know, I --
Tim Kazurinsky..

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:38.838 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yes, from Chicago as well.
-...brought me on --

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:42.041 align:start size:94% position:6%
or, suggested that I come on
and have a tryout.

00:05:42.041 --> 00:05:47.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, they put me in
that oversized suit

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:52.418
with a small little crew,
and threw me out onto the street

00:05:52.418 --> 00:05:54.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
to say, "Would you take
a bullet for the President?"

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:56.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Uh-huh.
-And pretty soon that --

00:05:56.823 --> 00:05:58.291 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was a little crowd
around us, you know.

00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:01.294
I didn't know what I was --
I'd never been to New York.

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:02.829 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Uh-huh.
-I didn't know where I was,

00:06:02.829 --> 00:06:04.831 align:start size:94% position:6%
what I was doing.
-That is such a terr--

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:07.100 align:start size:94% position:6%
That is the worst casting --
-The actor's nightmare.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
Right, yeah.
-That's worse than you as

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:12.071 size:78% position:39%
a 13-year-old niece.
-Right, right, right.

00:06:12.071 --> 00:06:13.239 line:92%
-But, it was so exciting.

00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:14.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
I -- well, I want --
I'll make sure you can see it,

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:16.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause you gotta go back
and watch this.

00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:17.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was really --
really fun to watch.

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:18.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Doug McGrath,
who's a wonderful guy,

00:06:18.945 --> 00:06:20.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
was also in that sketch,
as well.

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:22.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
So such an honor
to have you here.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:25.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm such a fan.
Thank you so much.

